{
  "finalText": "**B.**\n\nThe tell that made \"last time\" diagnosable is absent: both prior incidents had Redis connections pegged at 990+, and right now they're 230/1000 with normal memory — so the evidence already contradicts the Redis-leak hypothesis. A restart would be acting on the pattern's name rather than its signature; if the real cause is the 13:55 deploy (7 minutes before the 14:02 spike) or DB pool exhaustion, restarting Redis burns time, adds a variable, and may mask the actual fault. Thirty seconds to read the deploy log and DB pool costs almost nothing against a 40s p99 and either confirms a new cause or clears the field to restart with justification.",
